      "body": "## Summary\n\n`htmlCode.downloadUrl` and `screenshot.downloadUrl` returned by the Stitch MCP server (`get_screen` / `list_screens`) now redirect to the Google sign-in page for most screens. An agent authenticated only with a Stitch API key (`X-Goog-Api-Key`) can no longer download screen HTML or screenshots. This breaks the documented download step (`curl -L <downloadUrl>`) used by `react-components`, `react-native`, `stitch-loop`, `design-md`, `remotion` and other skills in this repo.\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Connect to the Stitch MCP server (`https://stitch.googleapis.com/mcp`) with an API key from Stitch settings.\n2. Call `list_screens` (or `get_screen`) on a project and take any fresh `htmlCode.downloadUrl`.\n3. Run `curl -sL -o out.html -w \"%{http_code} %{url_effective}\\n\" \"<downloadUrl>\"`.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe HTML file is downloaded. This worked earlier for the same project.\n\n## Actual\n\n- Without `-L`, the response is `302` to `https://accounts.google.com/ServiceLogin?continue=https://contribution.usercontent.google.com/download?...`\n- With `-L`, the final status is `200` and the final URL is `accounts.google.com/v3/signin/identifier?...`. The saved file is the Google sign-in page HTML, not the design.\n- Screenshots behave the same way: `https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/aida/...=w780` redirects to `accounts.google.com/v3/signin/identifier?continue=https://lh3.google.com/rd-aida/...`. The downloaded \"PNG\" is sign-in HTML.\n\nBecause the final status is 200, scripts that only check the HTTP status (for example `curl -f`) silently save the sign-in page as the asset.\n\n## What seems to have changed\n\nDecoding the `c=` parameter of the HTML download URLs shows two storage locations:\n\n| Storage in `c=` payload | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `aida_codefx / app_companion_generated_files / html_…` (older screens) | ✅ Downloads without sign-in |\n| `aida_codefx / stitch_files / stitch_html_…` (current screens) | ❌ Redirects to Google sign-in |\n\nIn one project with 23 screens (tested 2026-10-08, freshly fetched URLs):\n- HTML: 22 of 23 require sign-in. The only one that still downloads is stored in `app_companion_generated_files`.\n- Screenshots: 17 require sign-in, 2 still download, and 4 screens have no screenshot.\n\nURLs fetched a few days earlier, which downloaded fine at the time, now return `404`.\n\n## Impact\n\n- The MCP can still list screens and return design systems inline, but agents can't read the actual screen code or images. So the \"Stitch design → code\" workflows in this repo no longer work in headless or CI environments.\n- API key auth is the documented way to use the MCP, so there is no supported way for an agent to authenticate these download URLs.\n\n## Suggested fixes (any one would help)\n\n1. Make `downloadUrl` signed URLs that work without Google cookies again, as they did for `app_companion_generated_files`.\n2. Accept `X-Goog-Api-Key` on the download endpoints.\n3. Return HTML content inline (for example a `get_screen_code` tool, or an `htmlCode.content` field), and return screenshots as MCP image content.\n\nAlso, the download scripts in this repo could detect the sign-in page (check for `accounts.google.com` in the final URL or body, and check the PNG magic bytes) instead of saving it as the asset.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Stitch MCP via API key (`X-Goog-Api-Key`), remote server `https://stitch.googleapis.com/mcp`\n- Tested with curl from a Linux sandbox (no Google cookies)\n- Date: 2026-10-08\n",
